#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53b26ee4-53af-5660-a9e6-8e5cca8b3e1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5128d119-c06c-5daa-9e3b-d11bb41c4c21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5128d119-c06c-5daa-9e3b-d11bb41c4c21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248008} :answer-field/id #uuid "b494e80d-ad80-5ac0-93b8-45b7a1db27c5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248007}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f76e5fd-4f42-5a2b-bb6e-329cbea38e79" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-269990" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+x-6}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+x-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+3}{(x+3)(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x+3}}{\\cancel{(x+3)}(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx-2=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=2.$ \n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248009 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bf8a4b5-2e48-51ce-9fd7-b6e8f4b6c3e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248011} {:answer/id #uuid "68a605d8-5cbb-574b-aba6-af233cb8240e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68a605d8-5cbb-574b-aba6-af233cb8240e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248012} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dd1778a-d903-52e6-b178-078939c8c056" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248010}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9aa64d6e-83a2-5370-b8dd-754f74c2d8ac" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-270004" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{2{x}^{3}-2}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{2{x}^{3}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{2({x}^{3}-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-{3}^{2}}{2({x}^{3}-{1}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)}{2(x-1)({x}^{2}+x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the difference of cubes formula to factor the denominator.\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n(x-1)({x}^{2}+x+1)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $(x-1)=0$ gives the solution $x=1.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+x+1=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-4(1)(1) \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptote is $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53b26ee4-53af-5660-a9e6-8e5cca8b3e1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18d954d3-7da1-55ef-aafc-3da0ef6c6fa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18d954d3-7da1-55ef-aafc-3da0ef6c6fa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248015} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddbf5b17-be2f-5fbe-8a10-e2627bcddc0a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248014}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ee6e8ddb-5fef-526b-86e8-b1c6eaaae58c" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-278535" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{x-1}{(x+2)(x-1)}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-1}{(x+2)(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x-1}}{(x+2)(\\cancel{x-1})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx+2=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088128 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6f66990-06d9-576c-8ec8-0e851d7aef59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085700} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b6d8bb-933c-5659-9335-75b9258579f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085701} {:answer/id #uuid "ec7b5211-4e5b-5448-a564-63eca00461b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 9$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085702} {:answer/id #uuid "fe7f739b-97e9-5b80-9bf4-5f02c0d587f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-3, x = 0, x = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085703} {:answer/id #uuid "35b7c2ac-501a-53ac-9163-9e24b2ece8fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6b6d8bb-933c-5659-9335-75b9258579f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085701} :answer-field/id #uuid "d88d5db0-50d3-5ec5-ba52-5bb9ba9d98c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085699}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "683930f5-7231-57ef-90a9-236b0f94178e" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-4694" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\frac{2x}{x^{3} - 27}$." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}-27} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}-{3}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{(x-3)({x}^{2}+3x+9)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the difference of cubes formula to factor the denominator.\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n(x-3)({x}^{2}+3x+9)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $(x-3)=0$ gives the solution $x=3.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+3x+9=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={3}^{2}-4(1)(9) \\\\  & =9-36 \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dfe1985-d4cb-5492-bd5d-9558e920c0b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=0,\\,x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248018} {:answer/id #uuid "4863b7d9-e542-59d6-9d18-6b7e5cb3485a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\sqrt{2},\\,x=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248019} {:answer/id #uuid "ea841a13-1cac-51d7-b05c-de5daabcbf75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248020} {:answer/id #uuid "80f56930-3850-520c-9abd-ede0c6c067c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\sqrt{2},\\,x=0,\\,x=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248021} {:answer/id #uuid "fd852a2f-ac3c-5b80-9c7e-73a1924aef5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd852a2f-ac3c-5b80-9c7e-73a1924aef5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248022} :answer-field/id #uuid "42e10a13-8285-506a-bade-53788ddaf33a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248017}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d87d550-5004-5d22-b9a3-b2335e96082d" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-6913" :question/problem "Find all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}+2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}+2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{x({x}^{2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}({x}^{2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ does not have any vertical asymptotes."}]}
